            <ArticleTitle>[Phlebotomy-related complications. Introduction].</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>237-40</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Recently, management of the central phlebotomy station by the clinical laboratory has become the norm and phlebotomy is a routine job for many medical technologists. Although venipuncture-related accidents, such as nerve injury, unfortunately happen in a low but fixed ratio, there is little information to avoid this risk. This topic consists of three excellent reviews: Dr. Tanabe reviewed &quot;Iatrogenic peripheral nerve injury; mechanism and therapy&quot;, Dr. Ohnishi reviewed &quot;effective methods to prevent nerve injury at venipuncture&quot;, and Dr. Kitamura reviewed &quot;An effective and efficient method of painless venipuncture in children&quot;. I believe these three articles will be of great help to many medical technologists and clinical laboratory physicians to perform phlebotomy safely.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
